= Solution
{parent=a}

Using <Fourier inversion> with the stated convention,
$$
f(-\omega)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\widetilde f(t)e^{-i\omega t}\,dt.
$$
Hence applying the transform twice gives the <duality property of the Fourier transform>:
$$
\boxed{\mathcal F[\widetilde f](\omega)=2\pi f(-\omega)}.
$$
The formula holds directly for rapidly decreasing smooth functions, and extends through the usual <L2 space> or <tempered distribution> formulations when absolute convergence is unavailable.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a real nonzero $b$, put $s=bt$ in the <Fourier transform>. Reversing the integration limits when $b<0$ produces the absolute value:
$$
\boxed{\mathcal F[f(b\,\cdot)](\omega)=\frac1{|b|}\widetilde f\left(\frac\omega b\right)}.
$$
This is the <scaling property of the Fourier transform>. If $b=0$ and $f(0)$ exists, the transformed function is constant in time; its transform is $2\pi f(0)\delta(\omega)$ in the sense of <tempered distributions>, rather than an ordinary convergent Fourier integral.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <Heaviside step function> restricts the integral defining the <Fourier transform> to $t\geq0$, so
$$
\widetilde g(\omega)=\int_0^\infty e^{-(\alpha+i\omega)t}\,dt
=\boxed{\frac1{\alpha+i\omega}},\qquad \operatorname{Re}\alpha>0.
$$
The convergence condition is essential. For real $\alpha$ it means $\alpha>0$, which is the decay assumption used in the subsequent parts. At $\alpha=0$ a <distributional Fourier transform> is needed; for negative real $\alpha$ the defining improper integral diverges.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $\operatorname{Re}\alpha>0$, the even exponential is $h(t)=g(t)+g(-t)$ except for an irrelevant single value at zero. <Linearity> and the <scaling property of the Fourier transform> at $b=-1$ give
$$
\widetilde h(\omega)=\frac1{\alpha+i\omega}+\frac1{\alpha-i\omega}
=\boxed{\frac{2\alpha}{\alpha^2+\omega^2}}.
$$
In particular, for positive real $\alpha$ the transform is real and even, as expected from the symmetry of $h$.

= e
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Set $\alpha=1$ in part (d), obtaining $\mathcal F[e^{-|t|}](\omega)=2/(1+\omega^2)$. Apply the <duality property of the Fourier transform> from part (a):
$$
2\mathcal F\left[\frac1{1+t^2}\right](\omega)
=2\pi e^{-|\omega|}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\widetilde s(\omega)=\pi e^{-|\omega|}}.
$$

= f
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

For $T>0$ the difference of <Heaviside step functions> is an interval indicator, and $v=1/2$ on $(-T,T)$ and zero elsewhere. Its <Fourier transform> is
$$
\widetilde v(\omega,T)=\frac12\int_{-T}^{T}e^{-i\omega t}\,dt
=\boxed{\frac{\sin(\omega T)}{\omega}},\qquad
\boxed{\widetilde v(0,T)=T}.
$$
The apparent singularity at $\omega=0$ is removable. The formula remains valid for real negative $T$, because the original step-function difference and the oriented integral both reverse sign; for $T=0$ both vanish. This is the <Fourier transform of an interval indicator> and produces a scaled <sinc function>.

= g
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=g}

By <Fourier inversion>, the <sinc function> $\sin(At)/(\pi t)$ has <Fourier transform> equal to one for $|\omega|<A$ and zero for $|\omega|>A$. Thus the two bandwidths add to give
$$
\widetilde z(\omega)=\begin{cases}
2,&|\omega|<1,\\
1,&1<|\omega|<2,\\
0,&|\omega|>2.
\end{cases}
$$
The requested squared-magnitude <spectrum> therefore has heights four and one:
$$
\boxed{|\widetilde z(\omega)|^2=\begin{cases}
4,&|\omega|<1,\\
1,&1<|\omega|<2,\\
0,&|\omega|>2.
\end{cases}}
$$
Values at the jump points depend on the chosen Fourier convergence convention and do not affect any integral. The graph is shown below.

\Image[paper-1-q4-spectrum.png]
{title=Squared Fourier magnitude of the sum of two sinc signals}

The <Parseval identity> with this <Fourier transform> normalization gives
$$
\boxed{E=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}|\widetilde z(\omega)|^2\,d\omega
=\frac1{2\pi}(4\cdot2+1\cdot2)=\frac5\pi}.
$$

