= Solution
{parent=a}

A square <matrix> $A$ is a <diagonalizable matrix> over a specified field if some invertible <matrix> $P$ makes $P^{-1}AP$ diagonal. Equivalently, the underlying <vector space> has a <basis> of <eigenvectors> of $A$.

For example,
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&2\end{pmatrix}\ \text{is diagonalizable},\qquad
\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}\ \text{is not diagonalizable}}.
$$
The first is already diagonal. The second has only the <eigenvalue> one, of algebraic multiplicity two, but its <eigenspace> consists of $(x,0)^T$ and has dimension one. It cannot supply a two-vector <eigenbasis>.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Every row sums to six, so $\mathbf e=(1,1,1)^T$ is an <eigenvector> with <eigenvalue> six. Since $M$ is a real <symmetric matrix>, its other two <eigenvalues>, say $\lambda_2,\lambda_3$, are real. The <trace> gives
$$
\lambda_2+\lambda_3=\operatorname{tr}M-6=0.
$$
Also, for a real <symmetric matrix>, $\operatorname{tr}(M^2)$ is the sum of the squares of all its entries. Here
$$
\operatorname{tr}(M^2)=1^2+3^2+2^2+2(2^2+3^2+1^2)=42.
$$
Thus $\lambda_2^2+\lambda_3^2=42-36=6$. Together with their zero sum, this implies
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{spec}M=\{6,\sqrt3,-\sqrt3\}}.
$$

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

By the <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices>, choose an <orthonormal eigenbasis> $\mathbf u_1,\mathbf u_2,\mathbf u_3$ corresponding to $6,\sqrt3,-\sqrt3$, with $\mathbf u_1=(1,1,1)^T/\sqrt3$. Write $P=[\mathbf u_1\ \mathbf u_2\ \mathbf u_3]$ and $\mathbf x=P\boldsymbol\xi$. This <orthogonal change of coordinates> gives
$$
\mathbf x^TM\mathbf x=6\xi_1^2+\sqrt3\xi_2^2-\sqrt3\xi_3^2,\qquad
x_1+x_2+x_3=\sqrt3\xi_1.
$$
The plane constraint is therefore $\xi_1=0$. On that plane the <quadratic form> vanishes exactly when
$$
\xi_2^2-\xi_3^2=(\xi_2-\xi_3)(\xi_2+\xi_3)=0.
$$
Consequently the entire set is
$$
\boxed{\{t(\mathbf u_2+\mathbf u_3):t\in\mathbb R\}
\ \cup\ \{t(\mathbf u_2-\mathbf u_3):t\in\mathbb R\}}.
$$
The two direction <vectors> are nonzero and not parallel, since $\mathbf u_2,\mathbf u_3$ are <linearly independent>. An invertible <orthogonal transformation> maps each full line to a full line. Thus the set consists of precisely two infinite lines through the origin.

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
